+#### vLLM
+
+```bash
+servekit launch --servekit-artifact-path <dir> [--overlap] \
+  -- vllm serve <model> --tensor-parallel-size 4 ...
+```
+
+The same command shape, with vLLM's own `sharded_state` format (`vllm serve
+MODEL` or `--model MODEL`). What differs from SGLang:
+
+* The `--overlap` barrier is a vLLM plugin (`vllm.general_plugins`, installed
+  with servekit, inert unless `servekit launch` started the engine). It also
+  refuses a load that vLLM would narrow or cast tensors in, which is what a
+  checkpoint presharded for another TP size looks like, and it records every
+  rank's load time: vLLM logs `Model loading took` on local rank 0 only.
+* The model keeps its API name: `--served-model-name` is pinned to the original
+  path unless the command sets one.
+* Multi-node uses vLLM's multiprocessing backend: the same `servekit launch`
+  on every node, with `--nnodes / --node-rank / --master-addr` (and
+  `--headless` on the others). Each node stages its own ranks' shards and frees
+  them once all of its ranks have read them. Ray-placed ranks
+  (`--distributed-executor-backend ray`) run on vLLM's own loader, since
+  servekit cannot tell which node runs which rank.
+* vLLM's `sharded_state` stores weights after `process_weights_after_loading`
+  and runs that hook again on reload, so servekit does not preshard what this
+  cannot round-trip: quantized checkpoints, MLA models, expert parallelism, LoRA
+  and speculative decoding run on vLLM's own loader, with a log line saying why.
+  Check every new model with `servekit verify` before trusting it.
+* Compile caches (`VLLM_CACHE_ROOT`, Triton, TileLang, TVM-FFI, FlashInfer)
+  live in a node-local copy and are written back beside the checkpoint after
+  the first launch, so later launches skip torch.compile and JIT. vLLM's cache
+  key includes the served model path, which servekit keeps the same on every
+  launch.
 
 
